### What is the Two Pointers technique?

The two pointers technique uses two index variables that walk through a data structure, usually a sorted array or a linked list, to solve a problem in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. Instead of a nested loop that checks every pair (O(n squared)), two pointers reduce the work by making a single coordinated sweep.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class TwoPointerDemo {
    // Base pattern: two variables walking a structure
    // Variant 1: opposite ends moving inward
    // Variant 2: same direction, different speeds
    // Variant 3: fast/slow for cycle detection
}
```

### How does the opposite-end variant work?

Start with `left` at the beginning and `right` at the end of a sorted array. Based on a comparison, move one pointer inward. For a pair sum, if the sum is too small move left up, if too large move right down.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PairSumFinder {
    public static int[] findPair(BigDecimal[] sorted, BigDecimal target) {
        int left = 0, right = sorted.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            BigDecimal sum = sorted[left].add(sorted[right]);
            int cmp = sum.compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) return new int[]{left, right};
            else if (cmp < 0) left++;
            else right--;
        }
        return new int[]{}; // no pair found
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] settlements = {
            new BigDecimal("1000"), new BigDecimal("3000"),
            new BigDecimal("5000"), new BigDecimal("7000")
        };
        BigDecimal target = new BigDecimal("8000");
        int[] pair = findPair(settlements, target);
        System.out.println("Pair: [" + pair[0] + ", " + pair[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
Pair: [0, 3]
```

### How does the same-direction variant work?

A slow `write` pointer marks the boundary of the processed region, and a fast `read` pointer scans every element. When the fast pointer finds an element that meets a condition, it is copied to the write position and the write pointer advances. This does in-place compaction.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LedgerCompactor {
    // Remove zero-balance entries in place
    public static int removeZeros(int[] balances) {
        int write = 0;
        for (int read = 0; read < balances.length; read++) {
            if (balances[read] != 0) {
                balances[write] = balances[read];
                write++;
            }
        }
        return write; // new logical length
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balances = {500, 0, 300, 0, 0, 200};
        int newLen = removeZeros(balances);
        for (int i = 0; i < newLen; i++) {
            System.out.print(balances[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
500 300 200
```

### How does the fast/slow variant work?

One pointer advances one step at a time, the other advances two steps. In a linked list, if there is a cycle the fast pointer eventually laps the slow pointer and they meet. If there is no cycle, the fast pointer reaches the end. This also finds the midpoint: when fast reaches the end, slow is at the middle.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LinkedListCycleDetector {
    static class ListNode {
        int val;
        ListNode next;
        ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            if (slow == fast) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static ListNode findMiddle(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }
        return slow; // midpoint
    }
}
```

### What are the preconditions for each variant?

Each variant has a precondition that must hold for the approach to be correct.

| Variant | Precondition | Violation Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| Opposite-end | Sorted input | Silently wrong answers |
| Same-direction | Forward-only processing | Missed elements |
| Fast/slow | Deterministic advancement | Infinite loop or wrong midpoint |

```java
// Wrong: opposite-end on unsorted data
int[] unsorted = {7, 2, 5, 1};
// Pair sum search would give wrong results
// because moving left/right relies on ordering
```

### When should you use each variant?

The choice depends on the problem structure.

- **Opposite-end**: pair sum, 3Sum, container with most water, palindrome check.
- **Same-direction**: remove duplicates, remove element, merge sorted arrays, partition.
- **Fast/slow**: linked list cycle, middle of linked list, happy number detection.

```java
// Decision guide:
// Q: Is data sorted and looking for pairs?
//    YES --> opposite-end
// Q: Need in-place compaction or partitioning?
//    YES --> same-direction
// Q: Linked list cycle or midpoint?
//    YES --> fast/slow
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is applying opposite-end pointers to unsorted data, which silently gives wrong answers. Another is confusing same-direction with fast/slow: same-direction has both pointers under different rules, while fast/slow has a fixed 2:1 speed ratio.

```java
// Wrong: opposite-end on unsorted data for pair sum
int[] unsorted = {5, 1, 3, 7};
int left = 0, right = 3;
// Moving based on sum comparison is meaningless without order

// Correct: sort first, then apply opposite-end
Arrays.sort(unsorted); // [1, 3, 5, 7]
// Now opposite-end works correctly
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows all three variants and their preconditions, can pick the right one for a given problem in seconds, and explains why the chosen variant is correct. They know that opposite-end requires sorting, same-direction requires forward-only logic, and fast/slow requires a structure that supports deterministic traversal. They also know that two pointers can combine with other techniques (e.g., 3Sum combines opposite-end with a fixed-element outer loop).
